1
00:00:00,350 --> 00:00:04,877
Nobody outside the account sees Section 9.1's Wednesday step, or the scoring that

2
00:00:04,877 --> 00:00:06,050
happens every Monday.

3
00:00:06,050 --> 00:00:10,577
What a client, a boss, or your own future self actually sees is whatever makes it

4
00:00:10,577 --> 00:00:14,936
into a report, and that's a real gap between the work and how it's understood.

5
00:00:14,936 --> 00:00:18,401
This is where Section 8.2's honest ratio gets tested for real.

6
00:00:18,401 --> 00:00:22,760
It's one thing to write "noise" on your own scorecard where nobody's watching.

7
00:00:22,760 --> 00:00:27,286
It's a different thing to write it in a document going to someone who's expecting

8
00:00:27,286 --> 00:00:27,845
good news.

9
00:00:27,845 --> 00:00:32,428
The temptation to quietly upgrade a noise result into something more flattering is

10
00:00:32,428 --> 00:00:36,060
strongest exactly here, in the report, not in the scoring itself.

11
00:00:36,060 --> 00:00:38,519
A good report doesn't hide the honest ratio.

12
00:00:38,519 --> 00:00:42,990
It explains it, so the person reading it understands why most ad sets landing on

13
00:00:42,990 --> 00:00:46,399
noise or loser is the system working, not the system failing.

14
00:00:46,399 --> 00:00:50,814
Why does this explaining matter so much, rather than just reporting the numbers

15
00:00:50,814 --> 00:00:51,317
straight?

16
00:00:51,317 --> 00:00:55,843
Because a reader who's never sat through Section 8.2 has no reason to know that a

17
00:00:55,843 --> 00:00:57,464
mostly-noise batch is normal.

18
00:00:57,464 --> 00:01:01,991
Without that context, the honest numbers alone can read as failure to someone who

19
00:01:01,991 --> 00:01:05,511
doesn't already share this course's framework for judging them.

20
00:01:06,061 --> 00:01:09,302
Notice the right column isn't wrong information, exactly.

21
00:01:09,302 --> 00:01:11,519
Impressions and reach are real numbers.

22
00:01:11,519 --> 00:01:15,953
They're just not the ones that answer whether the account is making money, and

23
00:01:15,953 --> 00:01:20,501
reporting them alongside the CAC ceiling without that context lets a reader draw

24
00:01:20,501 --> 00:01:24,935
whatever conclusion flatters the numbers most, rather than the one the account

25
00:01:24,935 --> 00:01:25,958
actually supports.

26
00:01:25,958 --> 00:01:28,687
The fourth row is easy to skip and shouldn't be.

27
00:01:28,687 --> 00:01:33,235
A report that only ever shows this week's snapshot asks the reader to trust that

28
00:01:33,235 --> 00:01:36,248
progress is happening without ever showing the trend.

29
00:01:36,248 --> 00:01:40,568
Connecting this week to last week, even briefly, is what turns a report into

30
00:01:40,568 --> 00:01:45,002
evidence of a system rather than a single lucky result repeated on a schedule.

31
00:01:45,002 --> 00:01:49,664
Notice, too, that the left column deliberately mixes good and bad news in the same

32
00:01:49,664 --> 00:01:49,948
rows.

33
00:01:49,948 --> 00:01:54,496
Fatigue status includes a winner going tired, not just a winner performing well.

34
00:01:54,496 --> 00:01:59,157
A report that reads this way consistently signals something a highlight reel never

35
00:01:59,157 --> 00:02:02,455
can: nothing here is being filtered before it reaches you.

36
00:02:03,005 --> 00:02:07,368
Four lines, and every one of them traces back to a lecture you've already sat

37
00:02:07,368 --> 00:02:08,559
through this section.

38
00:02:08,559 --> 00:02:11,619
The counts come straight from Section 8.5's scorecard.

39
00:02:11,619 --> 00:02:13,319
Fatigue status comes from 8.3.

40
00:02:13,319 --> 00:02:15,983
What's queued next comes from 9.4's swipe file.

41
00:02:15,983 --> 00:02:17,399
Keep it short on purpose.

42
00:02:17,399 --> 00:02:21,537
A report stuffed with every number available reads as either padding or a

43
00:02:21,537 --> 00:02:24,994
distraction from one uncomfortable line buried in the middle.

44
00:02:24,994 --> 00:02:29,754
A short, honest report earns more trust than a long one, because there's nowhere for

45
00:02:29,754 --> 00:02:31,511
anything to hide behind volume.

46
00:02:31,511 --> 00:02:36,271
This same shape works whether you're reporting to a client, a boss, or just your own

47
00:02:36,271 --> 00:02:36,725
records.

48
00:02:36,725 --> 00:02:40,295
The version you write for yourself doesn't need softer framing.

49
00:02:40,295 --> 00:02:44,376
If anything, it's the one place brutal honesty costs you nothing at all.

50
00:02:44,376 --> 00:02:48,626
Fill in the third line even in a quiet week where nothing dramatic changed.

51
00:02:48,626 --> 00:02:52,933
"No change this period, still watching frequency on the current winner" is a

52
00:02:52,933 --> 00:02:57,467
genuine, honest line, and skipping it because nothing exciting happened is how a

53
00:02:57,467 --> 00:03:02,001
report quietly becomes a highlight reel without anyone deciding that on purpose.

54
00:03:02,551 --> 00:03:07,248
One honest trap worth naming, because it's genuinely tempting under real pressure to

55
00:03:07,248 --> 00:03:08,087
look competent.

56
00:03:08,087 --> 00:03:11,833
A report that only surfaces winners looks better in the short term.

57
00:03:11,833 --> 00:03:16,083
It also can't survive a single follow-up question about last month's quieter

58
00:03:16,083 --> 00:03:20,110
results, and the moment that question lands, every previous report looks

59
00:03:20,110 --> 00:03:23,185
retroactively dishonest, even the parts that were true.

60
00:03:23,185 --> 00:03:26,317
Trust, once it breaks that way, is expensive to rebuild.

61
00:03:26,317 --> 00:03:30,455
The honest ratio, reported consistently from the start, does the opposite.

62
00:03:30,455 --> 00:03:34,873
It sets the expectation early that most weeks are ordinary, so the weeks with a

63
00:03:34,873 --> 00:03:39,458
genuine winner actually stand out, and the reader already trusts the report enough

64
00:03:39,458 --> 00:03:40,968
to believe it when it does.

65
00:03:40,968 --> 00:03:45,106
Would you trust a report that never once mentioned a disappointing result?

66
00:03:45,106 --> 00:03:49,691
Hold that question against your own reaction the next time you're tempted to leave

67
00:03:49,691 --> 00:03:50,139
one out.

68
00:03:50,139 --> 00:03:54,836
Whatever answer you'd give as the reader is the same standard worth holding yourself

69
00:03:54,836 --> 00:03:55,787
to as the writer.

70
00:03:56,337 --> 00:03:58,419
Next lecture closes out this section.

71
00:03:58,419 --> 00:04:03,035
Then Section ten turns everything built here, the sprint, the economics, the swipe

72
00:04:03,035 --> 00:04:07,762
file, this report, into something you can pitch as a service, using the same honesty

73
00:04:07,762 --> 00:04:10,746
as proof of competence rather than something to hide.

74
00:04:10,746 --> 00:04:15,361
Before that, write one honest report on whatever's currently live in your account,

75
00:04:15,361 --> 00:04:17,331
even if nobody's asking for it yet.

76
00:04:17,331 --> 00:04:20,539
The habit is worth building before someone actually does.

77
00:04:20,539 --> 00:04:23,297
Keep every report you write, even the quiet ones.

78
00:04:23,297 --> 00:04:28,025
Section 10.3 turns this same record into a portfolio piece, and a redacted string of

79
00:04:28,025 --> 00:04:32,753
honest reports, ordinary weeks included, makes a far more convincing case study than

80
00:04:32,753 --> 00:04:35,061
a single highlight pulled out of context.

81
00:04:35,061 --> 00:04:39,732
Whoever reads it will trust the number precisely because the boring weeks are there

82
00:04:39,732 --> 00:04:39,957
too.
